total-derivative-principal-function.interpretation:
The equation 
\frac{dS}{dt} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{\partial\delta\delta S}{\partial q_i} \dot{q}_i + \frac{\partial\delta\delta S}{\partial t}
describes the total time derivative of a function $S$, which may depend on several generalized coordinates $q_i$ and time $t$. The term $\frac{dS}{dt}$ is the total derivative, accounting for all possible ways $S$ can change with time. The summation term $\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{\partial\delta\delta S}{\partial q_i} \dot{q}_i$ represents the contribution to the change in $S$ arising from the changes in the generalized coordinates $q_i$. Specifically, $\frac{\partial\delta\delta S}{\partial q_i}$ is the partial derivative of $S$ with respect to the generalized coordinate $q_i$, indicating how sensitive $S$ is to changes in $q_i$ at a fixed time. $\dot{q}_i = \frac{dq_i}{dt}$ is the time rate of change of the generalized coordinate $q_i$ (its velocity in the generalized sense). The product $\frac{\partial\delta\delta S}{\partial q_i} \dot{q}_i$ quantifies the instantaneous rate at which $S$ changes due to the motion along the $q_i$ direction. The final term, $\frac{\partial\delta\delta S}{\partial t}$, accounts for any explicit dependence of $S$ on time, independent of the changes in the $q_i$. This term is crucial when the function $S$ itself evolves with time, even if the coordinates were to remain fixed. In mechanics, if $S$ represents the action, this equation relates to the principle of least action and the Hamilton-Jacobi equation.
